Author = {Simmons, B. Alexander},
Title = {Eco-anxiety in Tampa Bay: Prevalence, risk factors and recommendations
   for environmental practitioners},
Journal = {PEOPLE AND NATURE},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {8},
Number = {7},
Pages = {2457-2480},
Month = {JUL},
Abstract = {As the environmental crisis grows, concerns over its impact on mental
   health are rising. A growing body of research is investigating novel
   ecological emotions, with eco-anxiety garnering significant attention.
   Yet much of the discourse has centred around clinical implications,
   leaving environmental practitioners feeling powerless to address
   experiences of eco-anxiety within their community. This study
   contributes to the emerging work on eco-anxiety and translates these
   findings into recommendations for environmental practitioners. A survey
   was distributed to a large, representative sample of the public and
   environmentally engaged ('in-network') residents in Tampa Bay, Florida
   (n = 1216), to identify sociodemographic, behavioural and psychosocial
   characteristics associated with affective symptoms of eco-anxiety.
   Linear and structural equation models were used to identify risk factors
   associated with progressive changes in eco-anxiety scores, as well as
   key differences in symptom severity classifications. Overall, 17\% of
   the public and 8\% of the in-network audience exhibit moderate-to-severe
   eco-anxiety symptoms. Subjective well-being was negatively associated
   with eco-anxiety. Well-being mediated the effects of age, education,
   place identity, solastalgia and other characteristics on eco-anxiety.
   Eco-anxiety was also negatively associated with perceptions of
   environmental justice, and positively associated with nature
   relatedness, negative experiences of change and engagement in
   pro-environmental behaviours. Residents' sense of response
   efficacy-based hope and their engagement in nature-based activities,
   however, were most predictive of the severity of eco-anxiety symptoms.
   Environmental practitioners are not powerless to assist community
   members on their path to wellness in this time of rapid environmental
   change. Approaches from clinical psychology interventions can be used to
   embed eco-anxiety coping tools into environmental practitioners' various
   messages, programs and activities to help build emotional resilience,
   foster social connection and illuminate problem- and meaning-focused
   coping strategies within their communities. Several primary and
   secondary enhancements are discussed that can be incorporated within
   environmental practitioners' existing toolkit, including experiential
   education, citizen science and volunteer activities.Read the free for
   this article on the Journal blog.},
